<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/paperwork-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/paperwork.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="512" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li>Appraisal lexicon</li></ol></nav><p class="tag">Reference</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Glossary: The Ohio Claim Lexicon</h1><p class="lede">43 terms you’ll meet in policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English and searchable.</p></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose"><div class="lex-tools"><label for="lex-filter">Search the lexicon</label><div class="lex-in"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="11" cy="11" r="7"/><path d="M21 21l-4.3-4.3"/></svg><input id="lex-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “holdback” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"></div><p class="hint" data-lex-count>43 terms</p></div><nav class="az" aria-label="Letters"><a href="#l-A">A</a><a href="#l-C">C</a><a href="#l-D">D</a><a href="#l-F">F</a><a href="#l-H">H</a><a href="#l-I">I</a><a href="#l-L">L</a><a href="#l-M">M</a><a href="#l-N">N</a><a href="#l-O">O</a><a href="#l-P">P</a><a href="#l-R">R</a><a href="#l-S">S</a><a href="#l-T">T</a><a href="#l-U">U</a><a href="#l-X">X</a></nav><dl class="lexicon"><div class="term" id="l-A"><dt>Actual Cash Value (ACV)</dt><dd>What damaged property is worth at the time of loss, usually figured as replacement cost minus depreciation. Many Ohio policies pay ACV first and the balance after repairs.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Additional Living Expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for the extra cost of living elsewhere while your home is unlivable after a covered loss, such as rent, hotel bills or added mileage.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Appraisal Clause</dt><dd>The policy provision that lets either party demand a panel of two appraisers and an umpire to set the amount of loss when the two sides disagree on price.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Award</dt><dd>The written decision that fixes the amount of loss. An award signed by any two of the three panel members is generally binding on the amount.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-C"><dt>Cosmetic Damage Exclusion</dt><dd>An endorsement on some policies that excludes dents or marks, often on metal roofing or siding, that change appearance but not function.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage A (Dwelling)</dt><dd>The part of a homeowners policy that insures the house itself and attached structures such as an attached garage or porch.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage B (Other Structures)</dt><dd>Coverage for detached structures on the property, such as a detached garage, shed or fence, usually set as a percentage of Coverage A.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage C (Personal Property)</dt><dd>Coverage for belongings like furniture, clothing and electronics, whether damaged at home or, within limits, elsewhere.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Coverage D (Loss of Use)</dt><dd>Coverage for additional living expenses or lost rental income when a covered loss makes the home unfit to live in.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-D"><dt>Declarations Page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing the insured, property address, coverage limits, deductibles and endorsements.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The share of a covered loss you pay before the insurer pays. It is subtracted from the claim payment rather than paid to anyone.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>The reduction in value for age, wear and condition. In a claim it is the difference between replacement cost and actual cash value.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Derecho</dt><dd>A long-lived, widespread windstorm produced by a line of fast-moving thunderstorms, like the June 29, 2012 event that swept central and southern Ohio.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-F"><dt>Functional Damage</dt><dd>Damage that impairs how a component works or shortens its life, such as hail that fractures shingle mat or cracks slate.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-H"><dt>Holdback</dt><dd>The recoverable depreciation an insurer withholds from the first payment until repairs are completed and documented.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-I"><dt>Ice Dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at a roof edge that traps melting snow, letting water back up under shingles and into walls and ceilings.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Impartial Appraiser</dt><dd>An appraiser with no financial stake in the outcome. Many policies require each party’s appraiser to be impartial, independent or disinterested.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-L"><dt>Line Item</dt><dd>A single entry in a repair estimate, such as removing and replacing a square of shingles, with its quantity, unit price and total.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-M"><dt>Matching</dt><dd>The question of whether undamaged materials must be replaced so repairs blend with what remains, common with roofing, siding and flooring.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps to prevent further damage after a loss, like tarping a roof or extracting water. Most policies require it.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Mortgagee Clause</dt><dd>The policy provision that protects the lender’s interest and is why claim checks are often made out to both the owner and the mortgage company.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-N"><dt>Named Peril</dt><dd>A policy approach that covers only the causes of loss specifically listed, such as fire, lightning or windstorm.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>NFIP</dt><dd>The National Flood Insurance Program, the federal program that writes most flood policies. Standard Ohio homeowners policies exclude flood.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-O"><dt>ODI</dt><dd>The Ohio Department of Insurance, the state agency that licenses insurers and enforces claim-handling rules.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Ohio FAIR Plan</dt><dd>The Ohio FAIR Plan Underwriting Association, the insurer of last resort for property owners who cannot get coverage in the voluntary market.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Open Peril</dt><dd>A policy approach that covers any cause of physical loss unless it is specifically excluded. Most Ohio homeowners policies insure the dwelling this way.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Ordinance or Law</dt><dd>Coverage for the added cost of bringing repaired property up to current building codes, usually capped at a percentage of the dwelling limit.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Overhead and Profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>The general contractor’s charge for managing a job with several trades, often figured as a percentage of the repair estimate.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-P"><dt>Percentage Deductible</dt><dd>A deductible set as a percentage of the dwelling limit, often 1%–5% for wind or hail on some Ohio policies, rather than a flat dollar amount.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Pitch</dt><dd>The steepness of a roof, written as inches of rise per 12 inches of run. Steeper roofs cost more to repair and may need extra safety labor.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Proof of Loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed that some policies require within a set time, often on request from the insurer.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-R"><dt>Recoverable Depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation the insurer will pay once repairs or replacement are finished, under a replacement cost policy.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Replacement Cost Value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with materials of like kind and quality, without a deduction for depreciation.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Reservation of Rights</dt><dd>A letter in which the insurer handles a claim while saying it may later deny coverage on stated grounds.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-S"><dt>Scope of Loss</dt><dd>The full list of damaged items and the repairs needed to restore them, which drives the size of every estimate.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Sewer and Drain Backup Endorsement</dt><dd>An add-on that covers water backing up through drains or a failed sump pump, usually with its own sublimit. Without it, most Ohio policies exclude this water.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Square (Roofing)</dt><dd>A unit of roof area equal to 100 square feet, used to measure and price roofing work.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Suit Limitation</dt><dd>A policy condition setting how long you have to file a lawsuit over a claim, often one year from the date of loss in Ohio policies.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request to add items or costs to an insurer’s estimate, often after hidden damage turns up during repairs.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-T"><dt>Test Square</dt><dd>A 10-by-10-foot area marked on a roof slope where an inspector counts hail hits to judge whether the slope is damaged.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-U"><dt>Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third member of an appraisal panel who decides items the two party appraisers cannot agree on.</dd></div><div class="term"><dt>Umpire Selection</dt><dd>The process of the two appraisers agreeing on an umpire, or, if they cannot, asking a court in the county of the loss to appoint one.</dd></div><div class="term" id="l-X"><dt>Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software used by most insurers and many contractors to price repairs from regional unit-cost databases.</dd></div></dl></article><aside class="side"><div class="side-box side-call"><h2 class="side-h">Talk it through first</h2><p>The first conversation is free. We’ll tell you plainly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="side-tel" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-white" href="/contact-our-team/">Send claim details</a></div><div class="side-box"><h2 class="side-h">Resources</h2><ul class="side-list"><li><a href="/appraisal-timeline/">Appraisal timeline<svg class="ar" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-q-and-a/">Appraisal Q&amp;A<svg class="ar" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-lexicon/" aria-current="page">Appraisal lexicon<svg class="ar" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-briefings/">Briefings<svg class="ar" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></li></ul></div></aside></div>
